    "description": "In a Hamiltonian graph G on n vertices with minimum degree at least 3, fix a Hamiltonian cycle C = v_0 v_1 ... v_{n-1}. A chord is an edge of G off C; two chords {v_a,v_b} and {v_c,v_d} are non-crossing if their endpoint arcs are nested or disjoint. It is known unconditionally that there are at least ceil(n/2) chords. Conjecture: one can always select at least c*n^{1/3} pairwise non-crossing chords. Proposed approach: model non-crossing chords as a laminar poset ordered by arc-inclusion and apply a Dilworth / Erdos-Szekeres antichain argument to a chord set of size ceil(n/2) with controlled crossing number. This is the missing bridge from 'many chords exist' to the constructive long-cycle construction.",
